 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to employee human trafficking recognition training and to authorizing the establishment of a standardized human trafficking recognition training program; requires public transportation services to require all employees to undergo a human-trafficking recognition training program established by the division of criminal justice services and the office of temporary and disability assistance in consultation with the department of transportation and the New York state interagency task force on human trafficking.",
 "title": "Relates to employee human trafficking recognition training and to authorizing the establishment of a standardized human trafficking recognition training program",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A1283",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A1283-2019",
     "printNo": "A1283",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 240\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 1283, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the social services law, in relation to employee\n      human trafficking recognition training; and relating to  authoriz-\n      ing the establishment of a standardized human trafficking recogni-\n      tion training program\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  necessitate  that  all public facing transportation\nemployees be required to complete a human trafficking recognition train-\ning program.\n \n  Human trafficking is a hidden crime and the first step to combating it\nis to identify victims so they can be rescued.  While  the  victims  may\nsometimes  be  kept  behind locked doors, they are often hidden in plain\nsight, and transported in the open by public transportation.\n \n  While I support the intentions of this bill, it would  duplicate  work\nalready  being  done.  The New York Interagency Task Force Against Human\nTrafficking has already developed the training that  meets  the  minimum\nrequirements of this bill, which is currently available for employees of\npublic  transportation  systems.  Transit agencies are, and will remain,\ncooperative. The task force will work to eliminate human trafficking and\nwill continue to participate in efforts to protect victims. However, the\ncore  mission  of  the  transit  agencies  is  transit,  and  to  divert\nresources,  when  the training already exists and is available, would be\npoor stewardship of limited available funds. Based on this reasoning,  I\ncannot support this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 240,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2019
   }
  ],
  "size": 1
 },
